  "evaluation": "B is best: it treats the supplied opposing findings as genuine, identifies the substantial population difference, and explicitly flags that Study A's harm direction is not supported by the quoted sentence. A makes essentially the same sound judgment and is a close second, with a slightly less precise caveat. C incorrectly calls the disagreement apparent based on sample size, power differences, and an unstated direction. Neither abstract identifies an underpowered study nor provides confidence intervals establishing compatibility, and both findings measure depression cases rather than different constructs.",
